{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Lifecycle policy properties in Amazon ECR","context":"## Tag status\n\n`tagStatus`\n: Type: string\n\n Required: yes\n\n Determines whether the lifecycle policy rule that you are adding\n specifies a tag for an image. Acceptable options are\n `tagged`, `untagged`, or `any`. If you\n specify `any`, then all images have the rule evaluated\n against them. If you specify `tagged`, then you must also\n specify a `tagPrefixList` value or a `tagPatternList` value. If you specify\n `untagged`, then you must omit both\n `tagPrefixList` and `tagPatternList`","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECR/latest/userguide/lifecycle_policy_parameters.html"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Examples of lifecycle policies in Amazon ECR","context":"## Filtering on image count\n\nThe following example shows the lifecycle policy syntax for a policy that keeps\nonly one untagged image and expires all others.\n\n```\n{\n \"rules\": [\n {\n \"rulePriority\": 1,\n \"description\": \"Keep only one untagged image, expire all others\",\n \"selection\": {\n \"tagStatus\": \"untagged\",\n \"countType\": \"imageCountMoreThan\",\n \"countNumber\": 1\n },\n \"action\": {\n \"type\": \"expire\"\n }\n }\n ]\n}\n```","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonECR/latest/userguide/lifecycle_policy_examples.html"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Clean up Your Container Images with Amazon ECR Lifecycle Policies | AWS Compute Blog","context":"## Tags\n\nCertainly 90 days is an arbitrary timeframe, and your team might have policies in place that would require a longer timeframe for certain kinds of images. If that’s the case, but you still want to continue with the spring cleaning, you can consider getting rid of images that are tag prefixed.\n\nHere is the list of rules I came up with to groom untagged, development, staging, and production images:\n\n* Remove untagged images over 90 days old\n* Remove development tagged images over 90 days old\n* Remove staging tagged images over 180 days old\n* Remove production tagged images over 1 year old\n\nAs you can see, the new Amazon ECR lifecycle policies are powerful, and help you easily keep the images you need, while cleaning out images you may never use again. This feature is available starting today, in all regions where Amazon ECR is available, at no extra charge. For more information, see Amazon ECR Lifecycle Policies in the AWS technical documentation.\n\n— Brent\n\n@brentContained","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/compute/clean-up-your-container-images-with-amazon-ecr-lifecycle-policies/"}]}}

## Recent AWS announcements (What's New)

Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon ECR Lifecycle Policies Adds Filtering Option for Tagged Images","context":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry (Amazon ECR) has updated its lifecycle policy feature to allow filtering of tagged images without requiring a specific tag value. Previously, creating a rule to filter tagged images necessitated specifying a tag value, making it challenging to select images tagged with automatically generated values, common in CI/CD pipelines","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2018/07/amazon-ecr-lifecycle-policies-adds-filtering-option-for-tagged-i/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Amazon ECR introduces archive storage class for rarely accessed container images - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) has introduced a new archive storage class to help reduce storage costs for infrequently accessed container images. This new feature allows users to meet compliance and retention requirements while optimizing storage expenses. ECR's lifecycle policies now support archiving images based on last pull time, enabling automatic archiving based on usage patterns. Users can also archive images individually using the ECR Console or API. Archived images do not count towards the image per repository limit and are inaccessible for pulls but can be easily restored within 20 minutes via the ECR Console, CLI, or API. All archival and restore operations are logged through CloudTrail for auditability. The new ECR archive storage class is available in all AWS Commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. For pricing details, visit the AWS pricing page. To learn more, consult the documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/amazon-ecr-archive-storage-class-container-images/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"EC2 Image Builder enhances lifecycle policies with wildcard support and simplified IAM - AWS","context":"EC2 Image Builder, a service offered by AWS for automating the creation, distribution, and management of customized Amazon Machine Images, has introduced two new features to simplify image management. Firstly, wildcard patterns are now supported in lifecycle policies, allowing users to manage images from multiple recipes within a single policy. This feature automatically applies lifecycle policies to all matching recipes, including new ones created in the future. Secondly, IAM role creation for lifecycle management has been simplified, with the console now automatically populating the required default permissions when creating a new role. Previously, users had to create separate lifecycle policies for each new recipe or manually configure permissions, making it difficult to manage image lifecycles at scale. These enhancements reduce setup time and potential configuration errors, enabling users to manage their image lifecycle more efficiently. Lifecycle Policies are available in all commercial AWS regions","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/02/ec2-image-builder-lifecycle-enhancements/"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"Amazon ECR now supports additional metrics for monitoring repositories - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) has introduced new repository metrics for monitoring through Amazon CloudWatch. These metrics, RepositoryCount and ImagesPerRepositoryCount, help users identify trends in image growth and repository creation and deletion. These insights enable users to detect anomalous behavior and receive alerts when their usage approaches the service quota. These metrics are available in all AWS commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions at no extra cost. For more information on Amazon ECR repository metrics, refer to the documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/02/amazon-ecr-additional-repository-metrics/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"aws-resilience-lifecycle","skill_description":"Guides the end-to-end AWS resilience lifecycle integrating Resilience Hub v2, Fault Injection Service, and Application Recovery Controller. Covers the Define → Test → Operate workflow: from policy creation through failure mode assessment, to FIS experiment validation, to ARC operational controls. Applicable when the user wants a complete resilience strategy, needs to connect findings to experiments to controls, or is planning a resilience program. Also applicable for the meta question of whether marking NGRH findings as resolved is enough, whether they are \"done\" after resolving findings, or how to validate findings before resolving them. Not applicable for resolving or remediating a specific individual finding (see resilience-hub-failure-mode-assessment), or when a single service is explicitly named (e.g. \"what FIS experiment should I run\").\n\n\nServices: resiliencehub, fis, route53-recovery-control-config, arc-zonal-shift\nTasks: configure, test, audit\nPersona: architect, sre, devops\nWorkload: resilience\n*","skill_name":"aws-resilience-lifecycle"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify how ECR lifecycle policy rules interact when multiple rules exist, re: tagStatus any catch-all", "parent_id": "90f5fdb6-5dd5-42c8-bdf0-a5ada7c10a12"}
